# Gitleaks false-positive fingerprints.
#
# Format: <commit>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# THIS FILE IS FOR HISTORY, NOT FOR THE WORKING TREE. Gitleaks scans every commit,
# so a finding stays reported after the offending line is removed — and rewriting
# published history is not an option here (force-push is forbidden in this project).
# So the rule is: fix the working tree forward, and fingerprint the commit that
# already shipped. An entry here should always be paired with a fix, never used as
# a way to keep a finding.
#
# Prefer a fingerprint over widening the allowlist in .gitleaks.toml. A fingerprint
# excuses exactly one line of one file in one commit; a path allowlist excuses
# everything in that path forever, which in a secret manager is the shape of hole
# that lets a real key through unnoticed.

# Two hardcoded high-entropy test passwords in the dynamic-credential provisioner
# tests. They were fixtures for the redaction assertions — the value a test proves
# does NOT reach an error, a log line or an audit row — so neither was ever a live
# credential.
#
# FIXED FORWARD: both now come from dynamic.NewPassword() instead of a literal. That
# is a better test as well as a clean scan, because it exercises the shape the service
# actually generates (full length, real charset) rather than a shorter hand-picked
# stand-in that could be redacted by luck. And it removes the underlying problem: to a
# scanner a fake high-entropy literal is indistinguishable from a real one, so keeping
# it would have required a permanent exception — and every permanent exception is a
# place a real credential can later hide.
